  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Diola\"\nsubtitle: \"Senegal\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Diola, also called Jola, are an ethnic group. |\n| Where | The Casamance region of southern Senegal and neighboring countries. |\n| How many | 475,000 speakers of the language. |\n| Language | Jola, also called Diola, Joola, Jola-Fonyi, Diola-Fogny, and Kujamataak. |\n| Religion | Not described in the profile. |\n| Known for | A rice-based dish · Hunting and fishing objects · Storage and cooking baskets · Palm-tree climbing equipment · Jola languages |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Diola are linked with the Casamance region of southern Senegal and neighboring countries. They are also called Jola, Joola, Jola-Fonyi, Diola-Fogny, and Kujamataak. The profile records many practical objects for hunting, fishing, cooking, storage, and domestic use. It also describes a rice-based dish called Thiebou Diola.\n\n## Material culture\n\nThe documented material culture centers on practical objects made from wood, palm fibre, bamboo, iron, vegetable fibre, cane, metal, cotton, plastic, hide, clay, and palm leaf.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nThe sources do not describe Diola clothing, but they record a palm-fibre belt used for climbing palm trees.\n\n- **Palm-tree climbing belt** — This belt is made of palm fibre and is used for climbing palm trees.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nThe records list practical objects for hunting, fishing, cooking, storage, and domestic use.\n\n- **Hunting and fishing spear** — This spear is used for hunting and fishing.\n- **Fishing basket** — This basket is used for fishing.\n- **Storage baskets** — The records include baskets for storage, including a basket for storing salt.\n- **Winnowing basket** — This basket is used for winnowing.\n- **Clay tobacco-pipe bowl** — The tobacco-pipe bowl is made of clay.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nThe sources do not describe Diola music, but a hide-and-wood drum appears in a museum catalogue.\n\n- **Drum** — The drum is made of hide and wood, but its name and musical use are not given.\n\n## Foodways\n\nThiebou Diola is a traditional rice-based dish associated with the Diola of Casamance.\n\n- **Diola rice dish** (*Thiebou Diola*) — Thiebou Diola, also called Ceebu Diola or “C'est Bon,” is a lesser-known variant of Thieboudienne with Diola-specific ingredients and flavours.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nJola is spoken in Casamance and nearby countries, with an official Senegalese writing system that marks long vowels and vowel harmony.\n\n- **Jola language** (*Jola*) — Jola, also called Diola, Joola, Jola-Fonyi, Diola-Fogny, and Kujamataak, is spoken by 475,000 people in the region and neighboring countries.\n- **Jola-Fonyi language** (*Jola-Fonyi*) — Jola-Fonyi is one of several closely related Jola languages spoken in the area.\n- **Official orthography** — Senegal created an official orthography for the language by decree No. 2005-981.\n- **Long-vowel spelling** (*aa, ee, ii, oo, uu*) — Long vowels are written by doubling the letter.\n- **Vowel-harmony accent** (*acute accent*) — An acute accent over a vowel marks the advanced and retracted tongue root for that vowel and the other vowels in the word.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Diola* — Name for the ethnic group and language\n- *Jola* — Alternate name for Diola\n- *Joola* — Alternate name for Diola\n- *Jola-Fonyi* — A closely related Jola language\n- *Diola-Fogny* — Alternate language name\n- *Kujamataak* — Alternate language name\n- *Thiebou Diola* — Traditional Diola rice dish\n- *Ceebu Diola* — Alternate name for Thiebou Diola\n- *C'est Bon* — Another name for the dish\n- *Thieboudienne* — Related rice dish\n- *aa* — Written long vowel\n- *ee* — Written long vowel\n- *ii* — Written long vowel\n- *oo* — Written long vowel\n- *uu* — Written long vowel\n- *acute accent* — Mark for tongue-root position and vowel harmony\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Bradley L. Hopkins, *Contribution à une étude de la syntaxe Diola-Fogny*, Cahiers de Recherche Linguistique, 4, Dakar: Société Internationale de Linguistique, 1995.\n- Bradley Hopkins and Elizabeth Hopkins, *Apprentissage de la langue Diola-Fogny: Un cours pratique*, Dakar: Société Internationale de Linguistique, 1992.\n- J. David Sapir, linguistic and folklore materials from the Kujamaat Jóola.\n- Wikipedia, “Thiebou Diola”: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thiebou_Diola\n- Wikipedia, “Diola language”: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diola_language\n- The supplied museum catalogue records include objects from the British Museum and the Museum of Ethnography.\n- No UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage inscription was supplied for this group.\n",
